enosys: refuse to load the filter under EXEC_DENY_INTERACTIVE

From: Skye Soss <skye@soss.website>

A seccomp filter decides what every system call made by the command
returns, including the prctl(PR_GET_SECUREBITS) and execveat() calls
that exec-aware programs rely on. enosys builds its filter from the
command line, so `enosys -s prctl -- python -c ...` turns off Python's
EXEC_DENY_INTERACTIVE check. Faking any other syscall, for example
`-s execveat:0`, can do the same.

Treat the filter as code given on the command line and refuse to load
it when EXEC_DENY_INTERACTIVE is set. --dump still works, since it
doesn't run anything: the dumped filter can be given execute permission
and loaded with `setpriv --seccomp-filter`, which checks it under
EXEC_RESTRICT_FILE.

